Keywords: Perovskite solar cell; solvent engineering; acetonitrile; cation-anion-mixed hybrid.
## 1. Introduction
Lead amine halide perovskite solar cells have been dominating photovoltaic research in this decade [1], owing to their lower cost, easier preparation, and higher efficiency compared with other photovoltaic devices [2-4], as well as the unique features of the lead amine halide perovskite ---- broad and strong light absorption, low exciton binding energy, and the long diffusion length and long life time of the charge carrier. The perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have obtained a great progress with a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $3.8\%$ in 2009 to more than $22\%$ at present [5,6]. Still, there is plenty of room to improve their power conversion efficiencies. Many technologies have been developed to improve the performance of the PSCs, including solvent engineering, interfacial engineering, bandgap engineering, etc [7-11]. Solvent engineering improves the quality of perovskite layers by optimizing the solvent compositions and regulating the crystallization process. Solvent engineering includes mixed solvents, solvent treatments, and deposition procedure optimization; it is an effective way to obtain high quality perovskite films, and is thus widely used [12].
Among various kinds of perovskite solar cell structures, planar sandwich configuration is widely used on account of its simple preparation procedure and low cost [13]. Lead amine halide perovskite is not only a light absorber; it can also function as the electron transporter and the hole transporter in the device. It is the most important component in perovskite solar cells. The anion-cation-mixed perovskite, such as the $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}(M A_{0.17}F A_{0.83})_{0.95}P b(I_{0.83}B r_{0.17})_{3}}$ [MA: methylammonium cation, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ ; FA: formamidinium, $\mathrm{HC}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})]$ , has become a research hotspot, owing to its high device efficiency and good stability [14]. The quality of the perovskite layer has a large impact on the photovoltaic performance of the planar perovskite solar cells. The perovskite film, with free-pinholes, fewer crystal defects, and fewer grain boundaries, can reduce non-radiative recombination and extend the charge carrier diffusion distance and life time [15].
In the preparation of perovskites, dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) and N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) are widely utilized, as polar aprotic solvents in precursor solution, to form Lewis acid-base adduct [16]. Some other solvents with lone pairs on oxygen, sulfur or nitrogen atoms are also used, such as $\gamma$ -butyrolactone (GBL), dimethylacetamide (DMA) [17], N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP), hexamethylphosphoramide (HMPA) and thiourea [18,19]. Solvents are removed as an intermediate by anti-solvent extraction or thermal annealing [20]. However, most of these solvents are toxic and high boiling point compounds. How to reduce the utilization of toxic solvents and develop low boiling point solvents is a significant challenge.
Acetonitrile (ACN) is a highly polar aprotic solvent, which can be used as a Lewis acid to form Lewis pairs with pervoskite precursors. However, the utilization of ACN is limited in a two-step method because ACN cannot dissolve PbI2. In order to improve the solvation of the perovskite precursor salts, Wu et al. introduced methylamine (MA) to slice the 3D $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ crystals into 2D layered perovskite intermediates. ACN can dissolve the layered intermediate phase, and high-quality $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite films were thus prepared and a device efficiency of $16.4\%$ was obtained [21]. Noel et al. bubbled MA gas into the ACN to form ACN/MA mixed solvents. Using the mixed solvent, a dense and pinhole-free perovskite film was prepared and a device efficiency of $18.2\%$ was obtained [22].
Here, we introduce ACN as an effective solvent additive in the precursor preparation of cation-anion-mixed perovskites. The addition of ACN not only lowers the boiling point of the mixed solution, but also acts as Lewis acid to promote the formation of Lewis acid-base pair $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ -ACN, thereby improving the crystallization process. Consequently, we obtain a compact, large grain and pinhole-free perovskite film. Based on the perovskite film fabricated by adding ACN, the device exhibits a high PCE of $19.15\%$ , which is better than that of the device without ACN additives. Besides, we replace toxic DMF with ACN in perovskite precursor solution, and replace high toxic carcinogenic solvent chlorobenzene (CB) [23] with green solvent ethyl acetate (EA) in anti-solution extraction. These replacements make the preparation of perovskite solar cells greener and more sustainable.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All of the materials were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Corp, China or Alfa Aesar Corp,
China, unless otherwise specified. 2,2’,7,7’-tetrakis (N,N-di-p-methoxyphenylamine)-9,9-spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) was purchased from Luminescence Technology Corp, Taiwan, China. Fluorine-doped tin oxide-coated (FTO) glass substrate with a sheet resistance of $14~\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ was obtained from Pilkington.
## 2.2. Perovskite precursor preparation
The perovskite precursor solution was prepared in a dry atmosphere in a glove box. 0.2 M MABr, 0.22 M PbBr2, 1.0 M FAI and 1.1 M PbI2 were added in a mixed solvent of DMF and DMSO [24-26]. After solid particles were completely dissolved, acetonitrile (ACN) solution was added, and the volume ratio of ACN/DMF was controlled at 0.0:16, 0.5:16, 1.0:16, 1.5:16, and 2.0:16, respectively. Meanwhile, 1.5 M CsI solution in DMSO was mixed in the above-mentioned solution, and the volume ratio of DMF/(DMSO $+$ ACN) was controlled at 4:1.
## 2.3. Solar cell fabrication
FTO glass substrates were etched by laser etching technology. The glass substrates were adequately cleaned with deionized water and ethyl alcohol. Then the FTO substrates were treated with ultraviolet and ozone processor for $35~\mathrm{min}$ . A compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ blocking layer was spin-coated onto $0.1\hat{2}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ glass substrate at a speed of $4000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $20~\mathrm{s}$ , followed by being sinterred in a muffle furnace at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ [27]. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electron transport layers were deposited on the clean FTO glasses by spin-coating the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ toluene solution at 4000 rpm for $30~\mathrm{s}$ , and then annealed at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 30 min [27].
The perovskite films were fabricated by spin-coating the perovskite precursor solution on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electron transport layers at 1000 and $6000\mathrm{rpm}$ for 10 and $20~\mathrm{s}$ , respectively. During spin-coating, $125~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of green solvent ethyl acetate (EA), instead of the frequently used highly toxic carcinogenic solvent chlorobenzene (CB), was dropped onto the glass for $5\mathrm{~s~}$ The resultant perovskite film was annealed at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $50\ \mathrm{min}$ on the hotplate. Thus a $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}(M A_{0.17}F A_{0.83})_{0.95}P b(I_{0.83}B r_{0.17})_{3}}$ perovskite layer was deposited on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electron transport layer.
A hole transport layer was fabricated by spin-coating $40\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of spiro-OMeTAD solution on the perovskite layer at $4000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathrm{s}$ . The spiro-OMeTAD solution was the mixture of $75\ \mathrm{\mg}$ spiro-OMeTAD, 1 mL chlorobenzene, $28.8~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ 4-tert-butylpyridine and 17.5 μL Li-TFSI solution $\mathrm{520~mg}$ Li-TSFI dissolved in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ acetonitrile). After placing in a dryer for $12\mathrm{h}$ , the Au electrode was deposited on the spiro-OMeTAD layer by using vacuum vapor deposition technology. Thus, the perovskite solar cell with a structure of FTO/TiO2/perovskite/spiro-OMeTAD/Au and an active area of $0.12~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (calibrated with a black metal mask of an area of $0.3{\times}0.4~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) was fabricated.
