## 3. Results and Discussion
## 3.1. Phase and morphology
Fig. 1 shows the X-ray diffraction pattern of perovskite films prepared with different ACN/DMF ratios. The main characteristic peaks of all samples at $14.12^{\circ}$ , $28.44^{\circ}$ , and $43.2^{\circ}$ are corresponding with the characteristic crystal planes (110), (220), and (330), respectively, which indicate the formation of the tetragonal perovskite crystal structure [28]. Obviously, with the increase of the ACN/DMF ratios, the intensities of the main peaks increase first; when the ratio reaches 1:16, the sample has the strongest diffraction intensity. Beyond the ratio value, the diffraction intensities decrease with the increase of the ratio, suggesting that an appropriate amount of ACN improves the quality of the crystalization process. On the other hand, it can be seen a small diffraction peak at $12.65^{\circ}$ , which is ascribed to a (110) crystal plane diffraction peak of $\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ , indicating the existence of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ crystals owing to the excess $\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ in the precursor solution. The excess $\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ contributes to the passivation of the surface defects and it can improve the performance of the device [29,30].
Fig. 2 $(\mathsf{a}\mathbf{\sim}\mathbf{g})$ displays top-view and cross-view FESEM images of perovskite films prepared with different ACN/DMF ratios. From the top-view FESEM images, it can be observed that with the increase of the ratio ACN/DMF, the pinholes of the samples decrease first and then increase. When the ACN/DMF ratio reaches 1:16, the film shows best quality; no pinholes can be observed and the grain sizes of perovskites are about $456~\mathrm{nm}$ . From Fig. 2 (a\~e), the grain sizes of perovskites prepared with the ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16 is significantly larger than the counterpart with other ACN/DMF ratios, indicating that ACN adding can affect the crystal growth and the grain size of the perovskite films; a larger grain means better crystallization and more dense arrangement [26]. The contact angles of perovskite films with different ACN/DMF ratios were measured and the results are inserted in Fig. $2\ (\mathrm{a{\sim}e})$ . The contact angles of perovskite films with ACN/DMF ratio of 1:16 is $79.16^{\circ}$ , higher than that of the films without ACN adding (64.42 º). The contact angles dramatically decrease after adding with excessive ACN.
The cross-view FESEM images of perovskite films with ACN and without ACN are shown in Fig. 2 (f, g). It can be seen that under the ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16, no obvious pinholes are observed and the perovskite film is almost constituted by a single layer grain, which suggests that the transport of charge carriers go through fewer grain boundaries [31]. Based on the results above, adding ACN can improve the morphology of perovskite films.
## 3.2. Photoelectrical properties
The ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) absorption spectra of five perovskite films prepared with different ACN/DMF ratios are displayed in Fig. 3. All the films exhibit obvious absorption in visible wavelengths ranging from $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $750\ \mathrm{nm}$ , but the absorption intensity are different. Notably, the perovskite film with an ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16 shows strongest absorption, compared with other films with different ACN/DMF ratios. Thus, the ACN/DMF ratio is closely related to the crystallization process, the grain size and the quality of perovskite films [12]. Results above suggest that the ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16 benefits the crystallization process and the quality of perovskite films.
Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) measurements were performed to investigate the mechanism of charge transport among the FTO/TiO2/perovskite films [32]. Fig. 4 (a) shows the steady-state PL of the perovskite films prepared with ACN/DMF ratios at 0:16 and 1:16. It is well known that the recombination of electron-hole pairs can release energy and produce PL emission. In general, a lower PL intensity indicates lower recombination of charge carriers, leading to a higher device performance. It can be clearly observed that there is a significant decrease in PL intensity for the film prepared with ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16, indicating that the charge carrier transportation takes place efficiently in a quality film.
The time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) decay spectra of the perovskite films prepared with ACN/DMF ratios at 0:16 and 1:16 were measured and shown in Fig. 4 (b). The spectra of perovskites at the peak emission ( $760~\mathrm{nm},$ could be fitted by a biexponential rate formular:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{I(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_{i}e^{\frac{-t}{\tau_{i}}}}\end{array}
$$
in which $A_{i}$ is the relative amplitudes and $\tau_{i}(i=1,2)$ represents the lifetimes of fast and slow recombinations, respectively [33]. The sample with ACN exhibits a charge carrier lifetime of $\tau_{I}=10.78$ ns (fraction $=19.17\%$ ) and $\tau_{2}=81.6$ ns (fraction $=80.83\%$ ), longer than that of the pristine samples with $\tau_{I}=5.1$ ns (fraction $=48.21\%$ ) and $\tau_{2}=30.6$ ns (fraction $=51.79\%$ ). The trap-assisted recombination for the defects of films is the main reason of PL decay. A longer lifetime of the film with ACN adding means fewer defects in the film, which is conducive to the transportation of charge carriers and to the performance improvement of the corresponding devices [34].
## 3.3. Photovoltaic performance
The current-voltage (J-V) curves of the planar solar cells based on the perovskite films prepared with different ACN/DMF ratios are showed in Fig. 5 (a), and the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are listed in Table 1. It can be seen that the short-circuit current density (JSC) values vary from 21.98 to $22.86\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and the open circuit voltage $(\mathrm{V_{OC}})$ values change from 1.06 to 1.09 V. The fill factor (FF) values of the devices are 0.74, 0.76, 0.77, 0.74 and 0.72, corresponding with the perovskite films prepared with ACN/DMF ratios at 0:16, 0.5:16, 1.0:16, 1.5:16 and 2.0:16, respectively. The devices with the ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16 obtained the best photovoltaic parameter, suggesting less potential loss of photoexcition dissociation and a more effective charge carrier transportation and extraction in the device with ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16 [35]. Consequently, the planar perovskite solar cells with ACN adding achieves a power conversion efficiency of $19.15\%$ , while the device without ACN adding gets an efficiency of $17.47\%$ at the same experiment conditions.
