Fig. 5 (b) exhibits the PCE statistics of one batch of 24 devices prepared with different ACN/DMF ratios. It can be seen that the sample with the ACN/DMF ratio of 1.0:16 achieves the highest PCE. Fig. 5 (c) shows the histograph of PCE values of one batch of 24 devices prepared with ACN/DMF ratios at 1:16. The highest PCE of $19.15\%$ and the average PCE of
$18.40\%$ are obtained. The histograph exhibits high reproductivity. The J-V curves of the champion device using ACN/DMF ratios at 1:16 measured by forward and reverse scans are shown in Fig. 5 (d). Notablely, these values slightly change through forward scan. Consequently, the PSC based on the ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16 exhibits slight hysteresis, which may be due to the dense and pinhole-free perovskite films.
The corresponding external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectrum of the device with a PCE of $19.15\%$ was measured, and the result is exhibited in Fig. 6. From the curve, it can be observed that the device can achieve a broad plateau of EQE values over $85\%$ in the visible range from 450 nm to 750 nm, demonstrating a high charge carrier transportation efficiency, which is consistent with the results revealed by the steady-state PL and time-resolved PL. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) obtained through the integration of the spectral reaches a value of $21.75\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which corresponds with the measured value of $23.58\%$ by the $J_{\cdot}V$ curve [36].
We measured the efficiency change of the same perovskite solar cell (ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16) exposed to air atmosphere for $40\mathrm{{h}}$ , and the results are shown in Fig. 7. It can be seen that the PCE values of the device have no obvious change and they remain at the same level of around $18.70\%$ , indicating that the device fabricated with an ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16 possesses stable efficiency output and good device stability.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, we develop a simple and green method to prepare mix-anion-cation perovskite $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}(M A_{0.17}F A_{0.83})_{0.95}P b(I_{0.83}B r_{0.17})_{3}}$ by introducing an effective solvent addictive of ACN. The introduction of ACN improves the crystal growth of perovskites, and a dense, pinhole-free, large grain, fewer defect and boundary perovskite film is thus obtained. Photoelectrical measurement indicates less electron-hole recombination and faster carrier transportation in the perovskite layers owing to ACN adding. The planar photovoltaic device based on perovskite layers prepared with the optimal ACN/DMF ratio at 1:16 achieves a stable power conversion efficiency of $19.15\%$ under one-sun illumination, while the device without ACN adding gets an efficiency of $17.47\%$ under the same experiment conditions. The facile process and excellent performance presented here render this ACN additive method promising.
